  "content": "Approach play is where the team loses the most strokes. Putting is second. Off the tee and around the green are close to the baseline.\n\n- **Wider read:** Over each player's newest ten rounds (55 rounds, 7 players, May 29 to Oct 21), the team averages -3.09 strokes per round on approach. Putting averages -3.03, around the green -0.10, and off the tee +0.40. The baseline is the PGA Tour at 0. There is no college figure for strokes gained.\n- **This season only:** The season read (Aug 21 to Oct 21) covers only 11 rounds by 1 player, so it is thin. It points the same way: approach at -4.37, putting at -2.40, and off the tee at -1.36. Around the green is +0.07.\n- **Coverage:** Audit Testplayer has no recorded rounds.\n- **What to do:** Most of the season data is missing for the other players. I'd go by the ten-round read and look at approach proximity by distance band to see which approach shots are costing the strokes. Putting by distance would do the same for putting. I can pull either.",
